Introduction
Part 8
completed a trade-authorization layer for a cooperating strategy. The strategy described its setup state, checked the active session, measured signal freshness, respected the global lock, and called the authorization function before placing a trade. That arrangement works because the strategy agrees to ask before calling OrderSend().
The trading account has a wider boundary. A trader can place an order from the desktop terminal, a mobile device, or the web terminal. Another Expert Advisor may use a different authorization system or none at all. A pending order may later execute because price reaches its trigger. A stop loss or take profit can close a position without the original entry program initiating a new market decision.
Those events are visible to the account even when they do not pass through the
Part 8
strategy. They require a different component.
An account statement records the financial outcome. It does not always show the sequence that produced it. During a later review, the origin of an order may not be immediately clear. It may have come from the desktop terminal, a mobile device, an Expert Advisor, or an automatic protective level. Terminal messages help during operation, but they are not arranged as a persistent activity journal.
This matters even more when multiple terminals, clients, or programs share one account. Without a common record, distinguishing intentional, accidental, and system-generated positions depends on memory and scattered terminal information. The problem is therefore not simply whether a trade exists. It is whether the trader can reconstruct how it entered the account and connect it to the other stages of the same operation.
Part 9 develops a Trade Activity Auditor. It is attached to one chart, but its transaction callback observes the trading account rather than just the chart symbol. It records supported live transaction stages and retains available references for correlating requests, orders, deals, and positions. These references do not independently identify a person, originating terminal, or particular EA instance.
The auditor does not judge the quality of the trading idea and does not interfere with an open position. Its role is to retain available transaction fields, observation time, reported reason categories, request results when supplied, and references to related trading objects. This gives traders a clearer activity history and gives developers a reproducible trail when transaction behavior must be investigated.
An Expert Advisor cannot receive callbacks while detached or while the terminal is closed. After restart, the auditor scans the configured history window and compares historical object identities with successfully persisted journal rows. It appends historical orders and deals not already represented by the relevant recorded object types. Callback records are marked LIVE; history-scan records are marked RECOVERED.
Defining the audit boundary
Keeping the auditor independent
The auditor belongs to the
discipline series
, but it does not include DisciplineLayer.mqh, DisciplineGuardian.mqh, or the example strategy from Part 8. It compiles and runs independently.
Responsibilities are divided as follows:
the authorization layer decides whether a cooperating EA may send its own request;
the activity auditor records supported notifications delivered to its terminal while running;
account history supplies available historical orders and deals within the selected recovery interval.
Live and recovered records
LIVE
describes an event received through OnTradeTransaction() while the auditor was running. It does not mean that the position remains open or that the trade was authorized.
RECOVERED
describes an order or deal discovered through a later history scan. It retains available historical object properties, not the original request/result context or missing intermediate notifications. An order still active at scan time is outside this history-only recovery path. This implementation applies symbol-policy classification only to live entry and reversal deals; retrospective classification would require a separately defined procedure and the effective historical policy.
Figure 1. Live capture and history recovery.
What is worth saving
The journal saves requests, order changes, deals, position changes, history transitions, recovered orders and deals, and optional symbol-policy violations. It does not save ticks, timer calls, or panel redraws. The stages created by one market order remain separate because they describe different account changes.
A market order shows why this distinction matters. Notifications can include order addition, execution deal, order removal, and entry into order history. Their arrival order is not guaranteed. A deal can change a position without producing a separate TRADE_TRANSACTION_POSITION notification, which concerns position changes unrelated to deal execution. These callbacks can arrive within the same second and may share an order or position reference, but they describe different stages. A deal records an execution, including a possible partial fill. Entry into order history can also represent cancellation without execution.
Collapsing the sequence into one row would hide useful evidence. Repeated copies of the same callback, however, would inflate the totals. The journal retains distinct supported stages and excludes timer refreshes. A bounded session cache filters repeated DEAL_ADD observations by deal ticket after successful persistence. Updates, position notifications, and requests remain separate observations; their row counts are not guaranteed to equal unique user actions.
Designing the auditor
Project structure
The project uses one Expert Advisor and four include files:
Figure 2. Project structure of the Trade Activity Auditor EA and its supporting include files.
TradeAuditTypes.mqh defines the event vocabulary and one complete journal row. TradeAuditJournal.mqh owns CSV reading, writing, period summaries, and exports. TradeActivityAuditor.mqh translates terminal transactions and history records into the common structure. TradeAuditPanel.mqh owns the chart objects and period controls. The EA creates these objects and forwards terminal events.
Each module has one responsibility: the auditor builds the records, the journal stores them, and the panel presents the selected results.
Figure 3 shows both capture paths. Each produces STradeAuditRecord, and the panel reads its totals from the journal.
Figure 3. Audit capture, storage, and review flow.
Event names describe account changes
The event enumeration follows the transaction model rather than using a generic TRADE label:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Defines the activity types recorded by the auditor               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_AUDIT_ACTIVITY
  {
   AUDIT_REQUEST =
0
,
   AUDIT_ORDER_ADDED,
   AUDIT_ORDER_UPDATED,
   AUDIT_ORDER_REMOVED,
   AUDIT_DEAL_ADDED,
   AUDIT_POSITION_CHANGED,
   AUDIT_HISTORY_ORDER_ADDED,
   AUDIT_HISTORY_ORDER_UPDATED,
   AUDIT_HISTORY_ORDER_REMOVED,
   AUDIT_DEAL_RECOVERED,
   AUDIT_ORDER_RECOVERED,
   AUDIT_DEAL_UPDATED,
   AUDIT_DEAL_REMOVED,
   AUDIT_POSTTRADE_VIOLATION
  };
The live history events should not be confused with startup recovery. HISTORY_ORDER_ADDED describes a live callback reporting that an order entered account history. ORDER_RECOVERED describes an order discovered by the later reconciliation scan.
One record for every output path
STradeAuditRecord carries fields used by the main journal, exported reports, panel summaries, and future analysis:
