recordTime is assigned using TimeCurrent() when a row is written. It represents the terminal's last known server quote time rather than an exact local persistence timestamp. Live eventTime starts with observation time; deal additions use the available execution time. Recovered deals use historical deal time, while recovered orders use completion time with setup time as a fallback.
Sorting by eventTime provides a historical view, but second-level timestamps and unordered callback arrival prevent exact sequence reconstruction. recordTime and captureMode distinguish immediate observations from later history scans.
Order, deal, and position references also remain separate. A deal ticket identifies one execution. Its related order ticket identifies the instruction that produced it. positionId holds a persistent position identifier resolved from position, order, or deal properties. positionTicket separately retains the callback or request position ticket. An unresolved identifier remains zero instead of being replaced silently by the ticket.
Some fields are naturally empty for certain events. A pending-order update has no deal profit. A deal created by a take profit may not carry request fields inside the callback. Empty strings and zero identifiers indicate unavailable references where the event does not supply them. Numeric fields must still be interpreted according to event type: a default zero does not by itself prove that a measurement was available.
accountLogin and server anchor each row to a specific trading environment. This is important when reviewing exports from multiple terminals because ticket numbers alone are not reliable account identifiers. The journal and state filenames also include account login and a filename-safe server label. Existing journal rows and state metadata are checked against the current account/server. A held lock handle coordinates ownership among instances using the same journal path, and an account change stops capture until the auditor is reattached.
Input_AllowedSymbols is empty by default, leaving the auditor in observation mode. When a list is supplied, a live entry outside it receives an additional POSTTRADE_VIOLATION record; no trade is closed.
MQL5 implementation
The implementation is divided according to responsibility rather than transaction type. TradeAuditTypes.mqh defines the record exchanged by every component. TradeAuditJournal.mqh stores and reads those records. TradeActivityAuditor.mqh creates them from live callbacks and account history. TradeAuditPanel.mqh presents journal summaries, while TradeActivityAuditorEA.mq5 connects the classes to MetaTrader events.
The main call path is short:
Figure 4. Main call path from transaction capture to panel update.
Recovery builds the same record structure from available historical objects. The shared storage layout does not make LIVE callback rows and RECOVERED object rows equivalent units of trading activity.
1. TradeAuditTypes.mqh: defining the shared record
