This file arrangement makes each dependency visible. The EA knows the auditor and panel. The panel knows the journal. The auditor owns the journal and produces the shared record. The journal knows only the record types. Terminal transaction details therefore do not leak into storage or chart-object code.
Operating the chart panel
The panel is a compact view of the journal, not a replacement for it. It displays:
auditor status;
selected period;
total journal rows;
violations;
live and recovered counts; and
up to three eligible order, deal, recovery, or violation records with the latest eventTime values inside the selected period.
Request, position, and history-transition stages remain included in the totals and CSV. They are omitted from the three chart rows so that technical callbacks do not displace the activity a trader is most likely to inspect.
The period controls are TODAY, 7 DAYS, 30 DAYS, and ALL. TODAY begins at midnight in the broker terminal's current time. The seven- and thirty-day controls use rolling windows.
DETAILS
expands the latest visible record with its strongest ticket reference, action, and note. Pressing it again collapses the line.
REFRESH
reconciles the configured history window against recorded object identities. It does not reload the chart, restore the missing callback sequence, or create a timer row.
EXPORT
creates a separate CSV containing rows within the selected eventTime period. Its filename includes the account login, broker date, selected period, and a counter used to distinguish exports:
MQL5\Files\TradeAudit\TradeAudit_<login>_<broker-date>_<period>_<counter>.csv
The period component is Today, 7Days, 30Days, or All.
Export creates a separate uniquely named file and leaves the journal unchanged. The source is opened before a target is created, and existing export names are not reused. Journal writes are attempted immediately, so a separate Save button is unnecessary; failures remain visible. Omitting a Clear Log control reduces accidental deletion through the panel but does not prevent external modification.
The timer refreshes displayed totals every two seconds without creating audit records. A manual REFRESH can still append previously unrepresented historical objects. LIVE and RECOVERED totals count different kinds of observations, not equivalent numbers of trades.
